1. e4 g5?! { This is the Borg Defence which is the 2nd most popular unorthodox opening vs. 1.e4. First is a6 the St. George Defence. This is less flexible for black as white can force black into a bad variation. } 2. d4 { This just gains space in the center. A very principle move in the opening which also comes with tempo on the g5 pawn. } { [%csl Gg5,Gc1][%cal Gc1g5] } 2... h6 { This move fails to ...h4! breaking up the pawns } (2... Bg7?! { This is called the Borg Gambit which is much like the Grob Gambit for white except black is down a tempo. } 3. Bxg5 c5 { [%csl Yd4,Yc5,Gg7,Gb2,Ra1][%cal Yd4c5,Gg7b2,Gb2a1] } 4. c3! { This move wasn't so great in the normal Grob Gambit but is possible since white has already played ...e4. } 4... cxd4 (4... Qb6 5. Bc1?! { This move is kind of dubious giving back the pawn and losing deevlopment. } (5. Nf3 { This was simply better and white is basically winning after } 5... cxd4 6. cxd4 $18 { [%csl Gd4,Gc5,Ge5,Gd5,Gf5,Ge4][%cal Gd4c5,Gd4e5,Ge4d5,Ge4f5] }) 5... cxd4 6. Nf3 Nc6 (6... dxc3?! 7. Nxc3 $16 { White is ahead in development and he lost his extra pawn but the development and piece placement still gives white a better position. } { [%csl Gc3,Gf3] }) 7. Bc4 d6 8. O-O Nf6 9. Re1 Bg4 10. h3 Bh5 11. cxd4 Bxf3 12. Qxf3 Nxd4 13. Qd1 O-O 14. Nc3 Rac8 15. Bd3 Ne6 16. Nd5 Qd8 17. Nxf6+ Bxf6 18. Bh6 Bg7 19. Bxg7 Kxg7 20. Re3 Kh8 21. b3 Rc5 22. Bc4 b5 23. Bd5 Nf4 24. Rf3 e5 25. b4 Rc7 26. Bb3 Rg8 27. Rg3 Rxg3 28. fxg3 Ne6 29. Qd5 Nd4 30. Rf1 Nxb3 31. axb3 Kg7 32. Qxb5 Qg5 33. Qd3 Qg6 34. Kh2 h5 35. h4 a6 36. Qxa6 Rc3 37. Rf3 Rc2 38. Qd3 Rc1 39. Rf5 Qe6 40. Rxh5 Qc8?? 41. Rg5+! Kh7 42. Qf3! { Mashinskaya, Iulia - Vlassov, Nikolai, 1-0, RUS Cup Qualifier 1, 2004, https://lichess.org/MvLonBiQ } { [%csl Gf3,Rh7,Rg7,Rg8,Rh8,Rg6,Rh6,Rh5,Gg5][%cal Gf3h5,Gg5g8,Gh5h8] }) 5. cxd4 d5 (5... Qa5+? { [%csl Ga5,Rg5,Re1][%cal Ga5g5,Ga5e1] } 6. Bd2! $18 { [%csl Gg1,Gf3,Gb1,Gc3,Gd2,Ge3,Gf1,Gd3,Gd4,Gc5,Ge5,Gd5,Ge4,Gf5][%cal Gg1f3,Gb1c3,Gd2e3,Gf1d3,Gd4c5,Gd4e5,Ge4d5,Ge4f5] }) 6. e5 { Gaining space and making the dark-squared bishop bad. } { [%csl Rg7,Re5,Gd4][%cal Rg7e5,Gd4e5] } 6... Nc6 7. Nc3 $18 { This is like a much worse version of the French Defence for black being down a pawn and with bad pieces. } { [%csl Gd1,Gf3,Rd5,Gc3,Rg7,Re5,Gd4,Rg8,Rf6][%cal Gd1f3,Gf3d5,Gc3d5,Rg7e5,Gd4e5,Rg8f6] }) 3. h4! gxh4 (3... g4 { This is an interesting try by black trying to sacrifice a pawn for development but we could simply take it or ignore it and have a better position. } 4. Nc3 (4. Qxg4 { Also a possible choice if you feel like defending positions for material rather than getting a simple advantage. } 4... d5 { [%csl Yc8,Yg4,Bd5,Be4][%cal Yc8g4,Bd5e4] } 5. Qf4 dxe4 6. Nc3 Nf6 (6... Qxd4? { This move may seem natural but fails to the tactical } 7. Nf3! { And black's best defence is } 7... Qd6 8. Ne5 f6 9. Nb5 Qxe5 10. Nxc7+ Kd8 11. Nxa8 Nc6 12. Qxe5 fxe5 13. c3 $18 { Ultimately white should be able to save his knight and the worst case scenario is black will trap the knight but by then white will win some pawns or accomplish something good in the time. } { [%csl Gc1,Ge3,Rd8,Ba8][%cal Gc1e3,Ge1c1,Gd1d8] }) 7. Nge2 { [%csl Ge2,Gd4,Rd8][%cal Ge2d4,Rd8d4] } 7... Nc6 8. Nxe4 Nd5 9. Qf3?! Ndb4 10. Qc3? { Van Kerkhof, D. - Basman, M., 1/2-1/2, Haarlem Masters 2018, https://lichess.org/0aYpMgu1 }) 4... d6 5. Nge2 Nc6 6. Be3 e5 7. Qd2 $16 { According to LEELA white is better. } { [%csl Ge1,Gc1][%cal Ge1c1] }) 4. Rxh4 d5 { Black should look to break up our center and ...d5 is the move to do that. } 5. exd5 (5. e5!? { A very interesting move which I call the Borg Advance Variation which does lose some of white's advantage but it gains space. Black should treat this like a Caro-kann and accept his worse version. } 5... Bf5 6. g4 Bg6 (6... e6 { Black attacks our rook here so we can't really take the bishop and get and advantage. } { [%csl Gd8,Rh4,Rg4,Rf5][%cal Gd8h4,Rg4f5] } 7. Rh3! { The rook can now control the 3rd rank. } { [%csl Gh3,Ga3,Gb3,Gd3,Gc3,Ge3,Gf3,Gg3,Gg4,Rf5][%cal Gh3a3,Gg4f5] }) 7. f4!? { Another interesting move which I consider the Ultra Advance Variation of the Borg Defence. } 7... e6 8. Nf3 c5 9. c3 Nc6 10. Be3 Qb6 11. Qc1 O-O-O 12. Nbd2 $10 { Here we get this really crazy position where a lot of pawns have been pushed and there is not much space so the position will favour the more positional player. This kind of looks like a French Defence which means white can't really get an advantage from this line. }) 5... e6 { [%csl Gd8,Rh4,Ge6,Rd5][%cal Gd8h4,Ge6d5] } 6. Nf3 { [%csl Gf3,Gh4,Rd8][%cal Gf3h4,Rd8h4] } 6... exd5 (6... Qxd5) *


